Use standalone’s Azure Artifact Signing hook on Windows so Electron Builder signs and verifies the app, native binaries, installer, and uninstaller as it packages them.
Allow up to 100 Windows payload files. The desktop x64 build currently ships 90.
Run tscircuit-after-pack.cjs after packing: it does what T3 Code's own hook does, then gives the updater a download folder of its own, so this app and T3 Code do not clear each other's pending updates.
Give the Linux packages names of their own, so the .deb neither replaces T3 Code's `t3code` package nor shares its `/usr/bin/t3code`, docs folder or update downloads: the package is `tscircuit-desktop`, maintained by tscircuit, and the program is `tscircuit-desktop-app`, because `tscircuit-desktop` is the command-line tool the app links for itself. The packaged name only changes on Linux; on Windows it decides where existing copies are installed.

